# Badges S (part 2): checked against official scouting.org excerpts.

@owner mb.scuba-diving
@complete complete
@note The Swimming merit badge must be earned before requirements 3 through 6, and requirement 4 is earned through a recognized scuba certification agency.
@desc Scuba Diving prepares you to dive safely: first aid and CPR for diving conditions, the Swimming merit badge as a prerequisite, the Scuba Diver’s Code, earning an open water certification, aquatic ecosystems, and careers in the scuba industry.
@safety Scuba training happens only with a certified instructor through a recognized agency, following Scouting America’s scuba policy.
1 demo! First aid and CPR :: First aid for diving-related conditions and when and how to perform CPR.
2 a The Swimming merit badge :: Earning the Swimming merit badge before the scuba requirements.
= mb.swimming.1
3 d The Scuba Diver’s Code :: The guidelines of the Scuba Diver’s Code and why each matters.
4 a! Open water certification :: Earning an open water diver certification from a recognized agency.
5 k Aquatic ecosystems :: What an ecosystem is and the aquatic ecosystems divers may encounter.
6 a Careers :: Careers in the scuba industry and researching one of them.

@owner mb.sculpture
@note The 2025 update reorganized these requirements; excerpts showed safety, sculpting options and a career option but not the full numbered list, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Sculpture introduces three-dimensional art: safe use of a sculptor’s tools and materials, modeling in clay, casting with plaster molds, visiting museums or studios, and careers in sculpture.

@owner mb.search-and-rescue
@note Search and Rescue was updated for 2026 and the excerpts’ numbering was inconsistent, so no requirement outline is published yet. Topics include hazards and first aid, staying found and signaling, maps and coordinates, kinds of SAR teams, the Incident Command System, and a practice search.
@desc Search and Rescue teaches how lost people are found: first aid for searchers and subjects, trip plans and signaling, maps, GPS and coordinate systems, how SAR teams and incident command work, and planning and carrying out a practice search.
@safety Practice searches are simulations with adult leadership; real searches are run by trained responders.

@owner mb.shotgun-shooting
@note Shotgun Shooting has general requirements and a choice of a modern shotshell option or a muzzleloading option, each with its own scoring standard. The excerpts did not show their numbering, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Shotgun Shooting teaches firearm safety and wingshooting: safety, laws and hunting ethics, shotgun sports, shotgun parts and care, the fundamentals of hitting a moving target, and qualifying with a modern or muzzleloading shotgun under a certified instructor.
@safety Shooting is done only on an approved range under certified range personnel, with eye and hearing protection, following the National Range and Target Activities Manual.

@owner mb.signs-signals-and-codes
@note Excerpts were partly garbled and did not show numbering, so no requirement outline is published yet. The badge emphasizes awareness of each system rather than mastery.
@desc Signs, Signals, and Codes explores how people communicate without ordinary speech or writing: Morse code, American Sign Language, semaphore and nautical flags, braille, sound and silent signals, trail signs, hand signals, traffic and map symbols, and secret codes.

@owner mb.skating
@note Skating has general requirements and four options: ice skating, roller skating, in-line skating and skateboarding. It was reorganized for 2026, and the excerpts did not show the new lettering, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Skating covers safety and first aid for skaters and one in-depth option: ice skating, roller skating, in-line skating or skateboarding, each with its own equipment, safety rules and skills.
@safety Wear a helmet and the protective gear your option calls for, and skate only where it is safe and allowed.

@owner mb.small-boat-sailing
@note Excerpts showed the topics (safety and Safety Afloat, rules of the road, weather and float plans, sailing skills and rescues, knots and lines, boat care and terminology) but not their numbering, so no requirement outline is published yet.
@desc Small-Boat Sailing teaches you to sail a small boat safely: hazards and first aid, Safety Afloat and the rules of the road, weather, preparing and rigging a boat, points of sail, tacking and jibing, capsize and person-overboard recovery, knots, and boat care.
@safety Sailing follows Safety Afloat with a qualified supervisor; pass the swimmer test and wear a life jacket.

@owner mb.snow-sports
@complete complete
@note Requirement 7 offers four options (alpine skiing, cross-country skiing, snowboarding and snowshoeing), each with its own steps.
@desc Snow Sports covers safe skiing, snowboarding and snowshoeing: hazards and cold-weather first aid, reporting accidents, trail markings, fitness, clothing and equipment, the Responsibility Code and avalanche safety, and one in-depth option.
@safety Ski and ride within your ability, follow the Responsibility Code, and never enter closed or avalanche-prone terrain.
1 k! Hazards and first aid :: Likely hazards and first aid for cold-weather and other injuries.
2 k Reporting an accident :: How to report an accident to ski patrol or emergency personnel.
3 k Trail markings :: The international trail-marking system.
4 demo Fitness :: Strength, endurance and flexibility, and exercises for your chosen option.
5 k Clothing and equipment :: The clothing and equipment for your option and how they keep you warm and protected.
6 k! Codes and avalanche safety :: The Responsibility Code, terrain-park safety and avalanche safety.
7 a An in-depth option :: One option: alpine skiing, cross-country skiing, snowboarding or snowshoeing.
